$DIR/main.p:14:35: Map calls f from several tasks at once, and the function literal passed here writes count
    Map starts tasks that call f, several at the same time. The function literal
    passed here writes count at main.p:14 with no lock held, so the calls would
    write it at the same time: a data race.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Use an atomic type for it, or lock a mutex around the write:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3406" for a longer explanation)
